{#
  "Find and locate guidance" — not designed yet, but this is the shape of its
  landing page: previous searches, so one can be picked up again, and a way to
  start a new one.

  Both tabs' rows are session-backed — recentlyOpenedDocuments/
  savedGuidanceDocuments, built in view-model.js from
  req.session.data.recentlyOpened/savedGuidance (each just an id +
  lastModified, seeded with the original 4/3 example rows the first time a
  session reads them, via app/data/guidance-lists.js — shared with v2's own
  /find-guidance, so a session's state is not namespaced per version), each
  id then looked up in app/data/guidance-documents.js for its title/version —
  rather than res.locals.guidedSearches/favouritedGuidance
  (app/data/guided-searches.js / app/data/favourited-guidance.js), which are
  still set for other pages that read them, but no longer drive this page's
  tables: neither of those older files has the version field these rows now
  show. Opening a document (GET /v5/find-guidance/document/:id) adds to/bumps
  recentlyOpened; "Save to search" on document-overview.html (POST
  /v5/find-guidance/save-to-search) does the same for savedGuidance — both in
  app/views/legacy/routes.js. "Open" goes via document-overview.html this time
  (/v5/document-overview/:id), the same stop-page a result on organic-search.html
  goes through, rather than straight to saved-document-view.html as it did
  when these rows came from guided-searches.js/favourited-guidance.js.

  Each row's "Remove" link carries its guidance-documents.js id and which tab
  it came from as ?id=/?tab= to /v5/find-guidance/remove-confirm — see
  delete-search-confirm.html — so that page can say which document, from
  which list, rather than falling back to a generic message, and so its own
  "Yes, remove" (a real POST) knows which of the two session arrays above to
  take that id out of.

  Saved documents (found by searching organically) used to have a second tab
  here, but are not shown on this page any more. app/data/saved-documents.js
  and saved-document-view.html are unchanged and still reachable from a
  result on organic-search.html — this page just no longer lists them.

  "Saved guidance" is a second tab alongside "Recently opened" instead — for
  a document saved directly rather than found through a search. Both tabs
  now share the same Title/Last modified/Version/Action column structure —
  no Last opened column (that field is gone entirely now these lists are
  session-backed — only id/lastModified are stored per entry) and no Steps
  left column, which favourited guidance rows used to show.

  "Recently opened" alone is capped at 5 rows — enforced in
  app/data/guidance-lists.js on req.session.data.recentlyOpened itself as
  entries are added, not just at render time — and its hint text states how
  many are shown, reading straight off the array's actual length, same as
  its tab count ("Recently opened (N)") below. It is a most-recent-first
  list (each open moves that document to the front). "Saved guidance" has no
  cap and its hint text is a fixed line rather than a count, but its tab
  count is the same dynamic array.length pattern.

  The back link comes from res.locals.backHref, set by the site-wide journey
  middleware in app/views/legacy/routes.js, following the pattern every
  other page here uses.

  This page mixes a two-thirds search box with a full-width tabbed table (two
  separate govuk-grid-rows) — it doesn't fit common/layouts/content.njk's
  single-column shape, so it extends common/layouts/base.njk directly and
  keeps its own grid markup, same as before this became a page module.
#}
{% extends "common/layouts/base.njk" %}

{% from "govuk/components/button/macro.njk" import govukButton %}
{% from "govuk/components/input/macro.njk" import govukInput %}
{% from "govuk/components/table/macro.njk" import govukTable %}
{% from "govuk/components/tabs/macro.njk" import govukTabs %}
{% from "govuk/components/tag/macro.njk" import govukTag %}

{% set pageName = "Find and locate guidance" %}

{#
  Just the number — the "Version" column header next to it already says what
  it is, unlike organic-search.html's cards, which have no such header to
  lean on and so still spell out "Version 1"/"Version 2" in the tag itself.
#}
{% set versionTags = {
  "Version 1": { text: "1", classes: "govuk-tag--green" },
  "Version 2": { text: "2", classes: "govuk-tag--purple" }
} %}

{#
  Both tabs build their table the same way, just from a different row list
  and a different Remove/Delete word — called twice below rather than
  duplicating this block once per tab.
#}
{% macro documentsTable(documents, removeWord, tabName) %}
  {% set rows = [] %}
  {% for doc in documents %}
    {% set titleCell %}<a class="govuk-link" href="/v5/document-overview/{{ doc.id }}?from=find-guidance">{{ doc.title }}</a>{% endset %}
    {% set versionCell %}{{ govukTag(versionTags[doc.version]) }}{% endset %}
    {% set actionCell %}
      <a class="govuk-link" href="/v5/find-guidance/remove-confirm?id={{ doc.id }}&amp;tab={{ tabName }}">
        {{ removeWord }}<span class="govuk-visually-hidden"> {{ doc.title }}</span>
      </a>
    {% endset %}
    {% set rows = (rows.push([
      { html: titleCell },
      { text: doc.lastModified },
      { html: versionCell },
      { html: actionCell }
    ]), rows) %}
  {% endfor %}

  {{ govukTable({
    head: [
      { text: "Title", classes: "govuk-!-width-one-half" },
      { text: "Last modified" },
      { text: "Version" },
      { text: "Action" }
    ],
    rows: rows
  }) }}
{% endmacro %}

{% set recentlyOpenedTable %}
  <p class="govuk-hint">Your {{ recentlyOpenedDocuments.length }} recently opened guidances</p>
  {{ documentsTable(recentlyOpenedDocuments, "Remove", "recently-opened") }}
{% endset %}

{% set savedGuidanceTable %}
  <p class="govuk-hint">Guidance you've saved for quick access.</p>
  {{ documentsTable(savedGuidanceDocuments, "Remove", "saved-guidance") }}
{% endset %}

{% block content %}

{#
  No breadcrumb — this is the "Find guidance" tab's own landing page (the
  top-level nav bar above already shows that tab as current), not a
  sub-page under it that needs a trail back to it. See organic-search.html/
  document-overview.html for pages one level in, which do show
  "Find guidance > ..." — and all-guidance-docs.html for the same choice on
  the "Manage guidance" side.
#}

<div class="govuk-grid-row">
  <div class="govuk-grid-column-two-thirds">

    <h1 class="govuk-heading-l">Find guidance</h1>

    <p class="govuk-body">
      Continue your search or start a new one.
    </p>

    {#
      A plain GET <form>, not the live client-side filtering
      organic-search.html's own search bar has (see [data-search-input]/
      [data-search-button] there) — this box only ever needs to hand a term
      off to that page, not filter anything itself, so a real form
      submission is enough: Enter in the input or clicking "Search" both
      just submit it natively, no JS required. q is read on the other end
      by GET /v5/find-guidance/organic-search (app/routes.js) to
      pre-populate and immediately apply that page's own search.

      Reuses .app-search-bar/.app-search-bar__input as-is (already shared,
      from _app-organic-search.scss) for the same GOV.UK-homepage-style
      input+button layout organic-search.html's own search bar already
      has, rather than introducing a second copy of that look.
    #}
    <form method="get" action="/v5/find-guidance/organic-search">
      <div class="app-search-bar">
        <div class="app-search-bar__input">
          {{ govukInput({
            id: "find-guidance-search",
            name: "q",
            label: { text: "Search guidance", classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" },
            formGroup: { classes: "govuk-!-margin-bottom-0" }
          }) }}
        </div>
        {{ govukButton({
          text: "Search",
          classes: "govuk-!-margin-bottom-0"
        }) }}
      </div>
    </form>

  </div>
</div>

<div class="govuk-grid-row">
  <div class="govuk-grid-column-full">

    {{ govukTabs({
      idPrefix: "find-guidance-tabs",
      items: [
        {
          label: "Recently opened (" + recentlyOpenedDocuments.length + ")",
          id: "recently-opened",
          panel: { html: recentlyOpenedTable }
        },
        {
          label: "Saved guidance (" + savedGuidanceDocuments.length + ")",
          id: "favourited-guidance",
          panel: { html: savedGuidanceTable }
        }
      ]
    }) }}

  </div>
</div>

{% endblock %}
